[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Offline Persistence & Local Cache Management in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Firebase Firestore provides robust offline persistence capabilities. When offline persistence is enabled, the Firestore SDK caches your document data locally. This allows your application to read, write, query, and listen to data even when the device has no internet connection."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling Offline Persistence"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Depending on whether you are using the Web SDK, Android, or iOS, persistence must be explicitly enabled in your initialization configuration. For the modern web modular SDK (v9+), you use the initializeFirestore method along with persistentLocalCache."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import { initializeFirestore, persistentLocalCache, persistentMultipleTabManager } from "firebase/firestore";

// Initialize Firestore with offline persistence and multi-tab synchronization
const db = initializeFirestore(app, {
  localCache: persistentLocalCache({
    tabManager: persistentMultipleTabManager()
  })
});"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Benefits of Local Caching"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Seamless Offline Access: Users can interact with the app and view previously loaded data without network connectivity."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimized Bandwidth and Cost: Firestore reuses cached data for identical queries, reducing the number of document reads from the backend server."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Local Mutations: Writes performed offline are queued locally and automatically synchronized with Firestore once the connection is re-established."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Cache Size and Garbage Collection"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because local storage is finite, Firestore automatically manages cache sizes using garbage collection. You can configure cache size thresholds to prevent excessive storage consumption on the client device."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import { initializeFirestore, persistentLocalCache, CACHE_SIZE_UNLIMITED } from "firebase/firestore";

const db = initializeFirestore(app, {
  localCache: persistentLocalCache({
    // Set a custom cache size or use CACHE_SIZE_UNLIMITED
    cacheSizeBytes: 104857600 // 100 MB
  })
});"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Clearing the Local Cache"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In scenarios such as user logouts, you may want to clear the local persistence cache to protect sensitive user data. You can accomplish this using the clearIndexedDbPersistence method."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import { clearIndexedDbPersistence } from "firebase/firestore";

clearIndexedDbPersistence(db)
  .then(() => {
    console.log("Persistence cache successfully cleared.");
  })
  .catch((err) => {
    console.error("Error clearing cache: ", err);
  });"}]
